plt.tight_layout()
      </input>
    </program>

    <image source="images/redline_pymc_fig7.png" width="80%"/>

    <p>
      Here are the posterior distributions for the key parameters.
    </p>

    <program language="python">
      <input>
az.plot_posterior(idata, var_names=['remaining_time'])
plt.tight_layout()
      </input>
    </program>

    <image source="images/redline_pymc_fig8.png" width="80%"/>

    <p>
      To compute the posterior distribution of the remaining time -- which is what we are primarily interested in -- we have to extract the samples of <c>gap_time</c> and <c>elapsed_time</c> and subtract them elementwise.
    </p>

    <program language="python">
      <input>
def get_sample(idata, var_name):
    """Extract a posterior sample from idata

    idata: InferenceData object
    var_name: string

    returns: NumPy array
    """
    return idata.posterior[var_name].values.flatten()
      </input>
    </program>

    <program language="python">
      <input>
gap_times = get_sample(idata, 'gap_time')
np.mean(gap_times)
      </input>
    </program>

    <pre>
8.754988603964444
    </pre>

    <program language="python">
      <input>
